  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 3121(a) and case law such as David E. Watson, P.C. v. United States, S corporation shareholder-employees must receive reasonable compensation for services performed before taking non-wage distributions. There is no fixed minimum — reasonable compensation depends on duties, experience, hours, and comparable industry wages.
Wrong (A): There is no statutory minimum salary of $100,000 or any other fixed amount. Reasonable compensation is determined based on facts and circumstances, not a bright-line threshold.
Wrong (C): Shareholder-employees who perform services are required to take reasonable compensation. The IRS will reclassify distributions as wages if no salary is paid.
Wrong (D): Distributions from an S corporation are not subject to self-employment tax. The issue is that wages must be reasonable before distributions can be taken free of payroll taxes.$EXPL$
