  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 707(a)(2)(B) and Treas. Reg. Sec. 1.707-3, contributions of property followed by related cash distributions within two years create a rebuttable presumption of a disguised sale. The partner recognizes gain as if the property were sold to the partnership at fair market value.
Wrong (B): The two-year presumption prevents automatic tax-free treatment. While contributions under Sec. 721 and distributions under Sec. 731 are generally tax-free, the disguised sale rules override that treatment when the transactions are related.
Wrong (C): Guaranteed payments under Sec. 707(c) are fixed payments for services or capital use. A property contribution followed by a cash distribution is not a guaranteed payment arrangement.
Wrong (D): The transaction is recharacterized as a sale, not a loan. A loan would require repayment terms, and the disguised sale rules specifically address contribution-distribution sequences.$EXPL$
