-- Q4179: Advanced Basis Calculations [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 705(a), outside basis is adjusted annually: increased by distributive shares of income (including tax-exempt income under Sec. 705(a)(1)(B)) and decreased by distributions under Sec. 733. Basis = $100,000 + $30,000 income + $10,000 tax-exempt income - $15,000 distribution = $125,000.
Wrong (A): $115,000 would result from excluding the tax-exempt income increase. Under Sec. 705(a)(1)(B), tax-exempt income increases basis to prevent double taxation on later sales or distributions.
Wrong (B): $140,000 would result from not subtracting the $15,000 distribution. Distributions reduce outside basis under Sec. 733.
Wrong (C): $130,000 would result from excluding either the tax-exempt income or miscalculating the distribution adjustment. All items must be included per Sec. 705(a).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4179;

-- Q4180: Advanced Basis Calculations [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 705(a), outside basis is increased by income and gain and decreased by distributions and losses. Basis = $80,000 + $20,000 ordinary income + $5,000 capital gain - $10,000 distribution - $35,000 loss = $60,000. Under Sec. 704(d), losses exceeding basis would be suspended.
Wrong (B): $50,000 would result from double-counting the distribution or misapplying the loss limitation. The correct calculation yields $60,000 with no suspended losses.
Wrong (C): $70,000 would result from omitting either the capital gain addition or miscalculating the loss deduction. All income and loss items must be applied per Sec. 705(a).
Wrong (D): $55,000 does not correspond to any correct combination of the basis adjustments. The proper computation is $80,000 + $25,000 income - $10,000 distribution - $35,000 loss = $60,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4180;

-- Q4181: Advanced Basis Calculations [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 752, a partner''s share of partnership recourse liabilities is included in outside basis. For recourse liabilities, allocation is based on economic risk of loss. In a general partnership with equal sharing, Partner C''s 25% share = $600,000 x 25% = $150,000.
Wrong (A): $0 would be incorrect because partnership liabilities increase a partner''s outside basis under Sec. 752. This liability share enables greater loss deductions under Sec. 704(d).
Wrong (C): $300,000 is 50% of the total liabilities, not Partner C''s 25% share. Each partner''s allocation is based on their proportionate economic risk of loss.
Wrong (D): $600,000 is the total partnership liability, not Partner C''s individual share. Only the partner''s proportionate share (25%) is included in that partner''s outside basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4181;

-- Q4182: Advanced Basis Calculations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Inside basis is the partnership entity''s tax basis in its assets at the entity level, tracked under Sec. 723 (for contributed property) and Sec. 1012 (for purchased assets). It is distinct from outside basis, which is each partner''s basis in their partnership interest.
Wrong (A): This describes outside basis — the individual partner''s basis in their partnership interest under Sec. 705. Inside basis refers to the partnership''s asset-level basis, not the partner''s interest-level basis.
Wrong (C): Fair market value is not the same as inside basis. Inside basis is the tax basis (adjusted cost), which may differ significantly from current FMV.
Wrong (D): Book value on financial statements follows GAAP or other reporting frameworks, not tax basis rules. Inside basis is a tax concept under the IRC, not a financial reporting measure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4182;

-- Q4183: Advanced Basis Calculations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The IRC Sec. 743(b) adjustment equals the transferee''s outside basis ($300,000 purchase price) minus the transferee''s proportionate share of inside basis (25% x $800,000 = $200,000), yielding a $100,000 step-up. This adjustment is personal to Partner D and applies only for D''s benefit.
Wrong (A): A $0 adjustment would apply only if outside basis equaled the proportionate inside basis. Here, Partner D paid $300,000 for a $200,000 share, creating a $100,000 difference requiring adjustment.
Wrong (B): $300,000 is Partner D''s total outside basis (purchase price), not the step-up amount. The adjustment is the difference between outside basis and the proportionate inside basis.
Wrong (C): $200,000 is Partner D''s proportionate share of inside basis, not the step-up. The adjustment is computed as the excess of outside basis over inside basis: $300,000 - $200,000 = $100,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4183;
